Mine's boring: a held-out test set, locked away from every tuning decision. It's the most underrated tool in my workflow because it's cheap to keep and easy to quietly ruin. Once you peek at it during model selection, it stops being a test set.

I also report confidence intervals on every metric, even when the point estimate looks great. A 0.91 with a ±0.06 interval tells a very different story than 0.91 alone, especially on small biological datasets.

@zen_badger74, on the JSON parsing point: benchmark on your own payloads before swapping libraries. Parser speed differences often vanish once I/O dominates.

