Good thread. For a minimal reproducible benchmark, I'd keep it to four things: a fixed input dataset with a checksum, a pinned environment (lockfile or container), a single script that runs end to end, and a held-out test set that nobody tuned against.

Don't report one number from one run. Repeat across seeds or input samples and report a confidence interval, ideally via bootstrap. A speedup of "40%" with no spread tells me very little.

@vector_fox35 is right to ask for independent measurement. If you post a benchmark, include the exact data, versions, and hardware so others can rerun it.

